insert into public.questions (id, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
('q5-001',5,'5.1','mcq',1,$q$A document states: All sensitive data must be encrypted in transit and at rest. Another specifies: Use AES-256 and TLS 1.2 or higher. What are these two documents, respectively?$q$,$q$["Procedure and guideline","Policy and standard","Standard and baseline","Guideline and procedure"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The high-level WHAT/WHY statement is policy; the mandatory specific HOW-MUCH (algorithms, versions) is a standard. A procedure would give step-by-step instructions; a guideline is optional advice.$q$),
('q5-002',5,'5.1','mcq',1,$q$Which document provides step-by-step instructions for enabling BitLocker on a corporate laptop?$q$,$q$["Policy","Standard","Procedure","Guideline"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Step-by-step operational instructions are procedures. Policy states intent; standards set mandatory parameters; guidelines recommend without mandating.$q$),
('q5-003',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$An employee is disciplined for cryptocurrency mining on a company server. Which signed document makes this enforceable?$q$,$q$["Business continuity plan","Acceptable use policy","Disaster recovery plan","SDLC policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The AUP defines appropriate use of company systems and, once acknowledged, grounds enforcement. BC/DR plans address disruptions; SDLC policy governs development practice.$q$),
('q5-004',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$Who is ACCOUNTABLE for deciding a dataset's classification and approving who may access it?$q$,$q$["Data custodian","Data owner","Data processor","Every end user"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The data owner (a business executive/manager) classifies data and approves access; the custodian/steward IMPLEMENTS the controls day-to-day; processors handle data on a controller's instructions.$q$),
('q5-005',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$Under GDPR-style privacy law, a company that decides WHY and HOW customer data is processed is the ___, while the SaaS vendor processing it on their behalf is the ___.$q$,$q$["processor; controller","controller; processor","custodian; owner","subject; controller"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The controller determines purposes and means; the processor acts on the controller's instructions. The data subject is the individual described by the data.$q$),
('q5-006',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the role of a Change Advisory Board (CAB) in governance?$q$,$q$["Writing application code","Reviewing and approving proposed changes for risk and impact before implementation","Conducting forensic investigations","Negotiating vendor contracts"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The CAB is the governance body of change management: assessing impact, dependencies, timing, and backout readiness before authorizing changes. The other functions belong to engineering, IR, and procurement.$q$),
('q5-007',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A risk assessment performed specifically because the company is about to launch a new payment platform is which type?$q$,$q$["Recurring","Continuous","One-time (project-based)","Ad hoc emergency"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$An assessment tied to a specific initiative is one-time/project-based. Recurring runs on a schedule; continuous is ongoing/automated; ad hoc responds to unexpected events or leadership requests.$q$),
('q5-008',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Ratings of High/Medium/Low likelihood and impact plotted on a heat map represent which analysis type - and its main limitation?$q$,$q$["Quantitative; too slow","Qualitative; subjective and hard to use for financial decisions","Quantitative; requires no data","Qualitative; legally binding"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Subjective scales are qualitative analysis - fast and intuitive but imprecise for justifying spend. Quantitative analysis assigns monetary values (SLE/ARO/ALE) for defensible cost-benefit comparisons.$q$),
('q5-009',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$A warehouse worth $2M would lose 30% of value in a flood. Floods historically occur once every 20 years. What is the ALE?$q$,$q$["$600,000","$30,000","$100,000","$12,000"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SLE = $2M x 0.30 = $600k. ARO = 1/20 = 0.05. ALE = SLE x ARO = $600k x 0.05 = $30,000 per year - the maximum rational annual spend to fully mitigate this risk.$q$),
('q5-010',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A company stops offering a risky legacy service entirely rather than securing it. Which risk strategy is this?$q$,$q$["Mitigate","Transfer","Accept","Avoid"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$Eliminating the activity that creates the risk is avoidance. Mitigation reduces it with controls; transfer shifts financial impact (insurance); acceptance documents living with it.$q$),
('q5-011',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Purchasing cyber insurance to cover breach costs is which risk strategy?$q$,$q$["Avoidance","Transference","Mitigation","Exemption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Insurance shifts financial consequences to another party - transference. The risk EVENT can still occur (unlike avoidance); controls are not added (unlike mitigation).$q$),
('q5-012',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$A startup accepts aggressive risks chasing growth; a bank accepts very few. Which term describes this organizational difference?$q$,$q$["Risk register","Risk appetite (expansionary vs conservative)","Key risk indicators","Risk reporting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Risk appetite is the amount/type of risk an organization is willing to pursue - expansionary, neutral, or conservative postures. The register catalogs risks; KRIs signal changes; reporting communicates them.$q$),
('q5-013',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Which document tracks each identified risk with its owner, likelihood, impact, and treatment status?$q$,$q$["Business impact analysis","Risk register","Statement of work","Audit report"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The risk register is the living catalog of risks and their management state. The BIA analyzes process criticality; SOWs define engagements; audit reports assess controls.$q$),
('q5-014',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$An e-commerce platform must be restored within 4 hours of failure, losing no more than 15 minutes of transactions. Identify RTO and RPO.$q$,$q$["RTO 15 min; RPO 4 hrs","RTO 4 hrs; RPO 15 min","MTBF 4 hrs; MTTR 15 min","RPO 4 hrs; MTTR 15 min"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$RTO is the restoration deadline (4 hours of downtime tolerated); RPO is tolerable data loss measured backward in time (15 minutes of transactions). MTBF/MTTR are reliability averages, not objectives.$q$),
('q5-015',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Which metric pair describes: average time a component runs between breakdowns, and average time to fix it?$q$,$q$["RTO and RPO","MTBF and MTTR","SLE and ALE","KRI and KPI"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$MTBF measures reliability (uptime between failures); MTTR measures repair speed. RTO/RPO are recovery OBJECTIVES; SLE/ALE are loss expectancies.$q$),
('q5-016',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$What does a Business Impact Analysis determine?$q$,$q$["Which vendor to select","Which processes are critical and the operational/financial impact of their disruption over time","Which employees to train","Which encryption algorithm to use"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The BIA identifies critical functions, dependencies, and how impact grows with downtime - producing recovery priorities and justified RTO/RPO targets that drive continuity investment.$q$),
('q5-017',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$Before signing with a payments vendor, the security team reviews the vendor's SOC 2 report and financial stability, and checks for conflicts of interest. What is this process?$q$,$q$["Right-to-audit exercise","Due diligence in vendor selection","Incident response","Data sovereignty review"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Pre-contract investigation of a vendor's security, viability, and independence is due diligence. Right-to-audit is exercised AFTER contracting; the others are unrelated processes.$q$),
('q5-018',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$Which contract clause lets a customer verify a vendor's security controls through inspection or by receiving independent audit results?$q$,$q$["Non-disclosure clause","Right-to-audit clause","Termination for convenience","Auto-renewal"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Right-to-audit turns vendor trust into verifiable assurance - inspections or third-party attestations on demand. NDAs protect confidentiality; the other clauses govern contract lifecycle.$q$),
('q5-019',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$Which agreement guarantees 99.9% uptime with defined penalties if missed?$q$,$q$["MOU","SLA","NDA","BPA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Measurable service commitments with remedies are the Service Level Agreement. MOUs are non-binding intent; NDAs cover confidentiality; BPAs define partner roles/profit-sharing.$q$),
('q5-020',5,'5.3','matching',2,$q$Match each agreement to its purpose.$q$,$q${"left":["SLA","NDA","MSA","SOW"],"right":["Measurable service commitments","Confidentiality obligations","Umbrella terms for ongoing relationship","Specific deliverables for one engagement"]}$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$SLAs define measurable performance (uptime, response times). NDAs bind confidentiality. The MSA sets master terms once, so each new project needs only a SOW/work order describing its specific scope and deliverables.$q$),
('q5-021',5,'5.3','mcq',3,$q$A vendor passed due diligence three years ago; since then it was acquired and outsourced support offshore. What third-party risk practice addresses this?$q$,$q$["One-time onboarding checks suffice","Ongoing vendor monitoring - reassessment, questionnaires, and review of material changes","Terminating all vendor contracts","Removing SLAs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Vendor risk changes with ownership, subcontractors, and incidents - continuous monitoring (periodic reassessments, updated questionnaires, breach-notification review) keeps assurance current. Point-in-time checks decay.$q$),
('q5-022',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Which are realistic consequences of regulatory NON-compliance? (Choose the BEST set.)$q$,$q$["Fines, sanctions, reputational damage, loss of license to operate","Faster audits and lower premiums","Guaranteed breach immunity","Mandatory marketing budget increases"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The exam's list: monetary fines, sanctions, reputational damage, contractual consequences, and potentially losing authorization to operate. Compliance failures never confer benefits or immunity.$q$),
('q5-023',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$A EU customer demands their personal data be deleted under GDPR. Which privacy right is this?$q$,$q$["Data sovereignty","Right to be forgotten (erasure)","Data minimization","Right to audit"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The right to erasure lets data subjects require deletion when no lawful basis remains. Sovereignty ties data to jurisdiction; minimization limits collection; right-to-audit is contractual.$q$),
('q5-024',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Employees sign annually confirming they have read the security policy handbook. What is this practice called?$q$,$q$["Attestation by the auditor","Policy acknowledgement","Certification","Accreditation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$User acknowledgement documents awareness of policies - supporting enforcement and compliance evidence. Attestation is a formal certification that requirements are MET, typically by management or auditors.$q$),
('q5-025',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Which statement about PCI DSS is accurate?$q$,$q$["It is a US federal law","It is a contractual industry standard for organizations handling payment card data","It applies only to banks","It replaces GDPR"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PCI DSS is imposed by the card brands through contracts with merchants/processors - not legislation. It applies to anyone storing, processing, or transmitting cardholder data, alongside (not replacing) privacy laws.$q$),
('q5-026',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Why can over-retaining data become a liability rather than an asset?$q$,$q$["Storage is expensive only","Retained data is discoverable in litigation and increases breach blast radius; retention schedules should delete what law no longer requires","Old data cannot be encrypted","Retention violates SLAs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Everything you keep can be breached, subpoenaed, and must be protected; retention policies keep data as long as REQUIRED and no longer. Cost matters but is not the core compliance point.$q$),
('q5-027',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$What distinguishes an AUDIT from a general ASSESSMENT?$q$,$q$["Audits are informal chats","An audit formally verifies controls against defined criteria with evidence; an assessment is a broader, often advisory evaluation","Assessments require regulators","Audits never produce findings"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Audits test conformance to explicit criteria (frameworks, regulations) and produce evidence-based findings - internal (committees) or external (independent third party/regulatory). Assessments evaluate posture more broadly and advise.$q$),
('q5-028',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$Which engagement provides INDEPENDENT assurance most valued by customers and regulators?$q$,$q$["Self-assessment by the IT team","Independent third-party audit or examination","Internal checklist review","Vendor marketing whitepaper"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Independence removes conflict of interest - external audits/attestations (e.g., SOC 2) carry weight self-assessments cannot. Internal reviews are useful preparation but weakest assurance.$q$),
('q5-029',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A red team attacks while the blue team defends, and both share findings in real time to improve detection. What is this called?$q$,$q$["Black-box testing","Purple teaming (integrated testing)","Compliance audit","Passive reconnaissance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Offensive (red) and defensive (blue) collaboration is purple teaming - the exam's integrated approach, maximizing learning per exercise. Box colors describe knowledge levels, not collaboration.$q$),
('q5-030',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$A penetration tester is given ONLY the company name - no network diagrams, no credentials. Which test environment is this?$q$,$q$["Known environment","Partially known environment","Unknown environment","Integrated environment"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$No prior information is unknown-environment (black box) testing - most attacker-realistic, but slower and less complete coverage. Known (white) provides full docs; partially known (gray) something in between.$q$),
('q5-031',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$Browsing a target's public website, LinkedIn profiles, and DNS records without touching their systems directly is what activity?$q$,$q$["Active reconnaissance","Passive reconnaissance","Exploitation","Lateral movement"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Gathering from public sources without direct target interaction is passive recon (OSINT) - undetectable. Active recon (scanning, probing) touches target systems and can be detected.$q$),
('q5-032',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$Before a penetration test begins, what document defines scope, permitted techniques, timing, and emergency contacts?$q$,$q$["Risk register","Rules of engagement","Data retention policy","BIA"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Rules of engagement (with written authorization) make the test legal and safe: what may be attacked, how, when, and who to call if something breaks. Testing without them is indistinguishable from attack.$q$),
('q5-033',5,'5.6','mcq',1,$q$Which awareness topic MOST directly reduces the top initial-access vector?$q$,$q$["Parking policies","Phishing recognition and reporting","Printer maintenance","Office ergonomics"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Phishing remains the dominant entry vector; training users to recognize lures and REPORT them (creating detection signal) attacks it directly. The distractors are not security awareness topics.$q$),
('q5-034',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$After a phishing simulation, employees who clicked receive immediate short training explaining the red flags they missed. Why immediate?$q$,$q$["Punishment works best fresh","Feedback closest to the behavior is most effective for learning; the goal is education, not discipline","To generate HR reports","Compliance requires same-day training"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Just-in-time teachable moments link the mistake to the lesson while context is fresh - behavior change, not blame. Punitive framing suppresses future reporting, which is counterproductive.$q$),
('q5-035',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$A colleague begins downloading unusually large volumes of customer data at odd hours and mentions resentment about a denied promotion. What SHOULD coworkers do per awareness training?$q$,$q$["Confront them directly","Report observations through the defined insider-threat reporting channel","Ignore it - not their job","Post about it internally"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Awareness programs teach recognizing insider-risk indicators (unusual access, disgruntlement) and reporting through proper channels for professional evaluation. Confrontation tips off the person; silence enables harm.$q$),
('q5-036',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$Which practices belong in remote/hybrid work security guidance? (Choose the BEST set.)$q$,$q$["Securing home Wi-Fi, screen privacy in public, VPN use, locking devices","Disabling all updates while traveling","Using public kiosks for corporate email","Sharing the work laptop with family"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$Hybrid work guidance: harden the home network, prevent shoulder surfing, use the VPN, physically secure devices. The other options are exactly what training warns against.$q$),
('q5-037',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$Which plan ensures ESSENTIAL functions continue DURING a disruption, as distinct from restoring IT systems after one?$q$,$q$["Disaster recovery plan","Business continuity / continuity of operations plan","Incident response plan","SDLC policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$BC/COOP keeps the business functioning (alternate processes, sites, staffing) through disruption; DR is the IT-restoration subset; IR handles security incidents specifically.$q$),
('q5-038',5,'5.1','mcq',3,$q$A multinational must reconcile GDPR, US state privacy laws, and local data-localization rules. Which governance consideration does this illustrate?$q$,$q$["Board committee structure","Jurisdictional complexity: local/regional/national/global requirements can conflict and all bind the organization","Password standard selection","Change management windows"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Governance must satisfy every jurisdiction touching the data - laws differ and sometimes conflict (localization vs cross-border transfer), driving architecture and legal strategy. The other options are internal matters.$q$),
('q5-039',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Phishing click rate rising three quarters in a row appears on the security dashboard as an early warning. What is this metric an example of?$q$,$q$["Key risk indicator","ALE","RTO","Attestation"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$KRIs are measurable signals that risk exposure is trending up, prompting action before incidents. ALE quantifies expected loss; RTO is a recovery objective; attestation certifies compliance.$q$),
('q5-040',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Who should own the risk of an unpatched business-critical ERP system?$q$,$q$["The SOC analyst who found it","The business executive accountable for the ERP-supported process","The intern who runs scans","The vendor only"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Risk ownership sits with whoever is accountable for the affected business outcome and can authorize treatment (spend, downtime, acceptance) - not with the analysts who report it. Vendors share responsibility contractually but cannot own your business risk.$q$),
('q5-041',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$Internal compliance reporting differs from external in that it...$q$,$q$["is legally binding on regulators","informs leadership/board of control status for decisions, while external reporting satisfies regulators, auditors, or customers","never uses metrics","replaces audits"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Internal reporting drives governance decisions; external reporting fulfills legal/contractual obligations to outside parties. Both use metrics; neither replaces audits.$q$),
('q5-042',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$Which testing type examines PHYSICAL security - attempting to tailgate, pick locks, or bypass badge readers?$q$,$q$["Defensive pen testing","Physical penetration testing","Regulatory examination","Static analysis"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Physical pen tests attack facility controls: entry points, guards, sensors, mantraps. Defensive testing exercises the blue team; examinations review evidence; static analysis reads code.$q$),
('q5-043',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$How should the EFFECTIVENESS of a security awareness program be measured?$q$,$q$["Count of posters hung","Trend metrics: phishing simulation click/report rates, user-reported incidents, training completion","Number of firewall rules","Antivirus version numbers"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Awareness effectiveness shows up in BEHAVIOR: falling click rates, rising report rates, more user-caught incidents. Execution metrics (completion) plus outcome metrics (behavior) together tell the story.$q$),
('q5-044',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the governance difference between centralized and decentralized structures?$q$,$q$["Centralized has no policies","Centralized concentrates security decisions in one body for consistency; decentralized delegates to business units for speed/local fit at the cost of consistency","Decentralized is always more secure","They differ only in budget"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Centralized governance yields uniform policy and clearer accountability; decentralized adapts faster locally but risks inconsistency. Neither is universally superior - the exam wants the trade-off.$q$),
('q5-045',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$Risk tolerance vs risk appetite: a company has appetite for cloud innovation but sets a tolerance that no single incident may cause more than $50k loss. Which statement is correct?$q$,$q$["Appetite and tolerance are identical","Appetite is the broad willingness to take risk; tolerance sets acceptable deviation/thresholds around specific objectives","Tolerance always exceeds appetite","Appetite applies only to finance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Appetite is the strategic posture (how much risk we WANT to take); tolerance operationalizes it as measurable boundaries per objective ($50k cap). They work together: appetite guides, tolerance constrains.$q$),
('q5-046',5,'5.5','mcq',2,$q$An organization receives a SOC 2 Type II report from its cloud vendor. What does this represent?$q$,$q$["The vendor's marketing claims","An independent attestation that the vendor's controls operated effectively over a period","A penetration test report","A government license"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SOC 2 Type II is an independent auditor's attestation on control design AND operating effectiveness over time (Type I is design at a point in time) - a cornerstone of third-party assurance.$q$),
('q5-047',5,'5.6','mcq',2,$q$What is OPSEC in user behavior terms?$q$,$q$["Encrypting the operating system","Everyday habits that protect sensitive information: clean desk, screen locking, discretion in public and on social media","A firewall configuration","An audit framework"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Operational security is behavioral: denying adversaries free information through disciplined daily habits - what you leave visible, say aloud, or post. It complements technical controls.$q$),
('q5-048',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$Which policy governs how software is designed, coded, tested, and released securely?$q$,$q$["Acceptable use policy","Software development lifecycle (SDLC) policy","Business continuity policy","Physical security policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SDLC policy embeds security through development: requirements, secure coding standards, review/testing gates, release approval. AUP governs users; BC and physical policies cover other domains.$q$),
('q5-049',5,'5.2','ordering',3,$q$Order these quantitative risk steps from first to last.$q$,$q$["Determine asset value","Determine exposure factor","Calculate SLE","Estimate ARO","Calculate ALE"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Asset value x exposure factor = SLE (loss per event); estimating frequency gives ARO; SLE x ARO = ALE (expected annual loss) - which justifies control spending in financial terms leadership understands.$q$),
('q5-050',5,'5.3','mcq',2,$q$Why does supply chain analysis extend BEYOND your direct vendor?$q$,$q$["First-tier vendors are always secure","Your vendor's suppliers and subcontractors inherit risk transitively - a fourth-party breach can still reach you","Regulations only cover direct contracts","It reduces contract costs"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Risk flows through the chain: your vendor's hosting provider, component suppliers, or support subcontractors can be the weak link (fourth-party risk). Analysis maps critical dependencies past tier one.$q$);
